## What a model is made of

<At levels="simple">

So far, text goes in as tokens, each token becomes a list of numbers, and out comes a chance for
every possible next token. A language model is the calculation in between. This Part asks what that
calculation is made of (this chapter) and where its numbers come from (the next one).

The counting model in <ChapterRef to="predicting-by-counting" /> looked its answer up in a table.
A large language model works it out instead, by passing the numbers through millions of tiny
calculators called <GlossaryPopover term="neuron">neurons</GlossaryPopover>.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The pieces so far fit together like this. Text is split into tokens
(<ChapterRef to="tokens" />), each token becomes a vector, a list of numbers
(<ChapterRef to="embeddings" />), and at the other end the model gives a chance for every possible
next token (<ChapterRef to="probability-and-sampling" />). A language model is the calculation in
between: numbers in, chances out. This Part answers two questions about it. What is the calculation
made of? That's this chapter. And where do its numbers come from? That's the next.

The counting model in <ChapterRef to="predicting-by-counting" /> computed almost nothing: it looked
its answer up in a table of counts. A large language model computes its answer instead, by passing
the numbers through millions of tiny calculators called <GlossaryPopover term="neuron">neurons</GlossaryPopover>.

</At>

## One neuron

Each neuron is simple enough to work out by hand. This one has two inputs so you can follow every
number; a neuron in GPT-2 reads 768. Move the sliders and watch the numbers flow.

<NeuronPlayground />

## Multiply, add, squash

<At levels="simple">

A neuron does three things. It multiplies each input by its own <GlossaryPopover term="weight">weight</GlossaryPopover>,
which says how much that input matters. It adds the results up, plus one extra number called the
**bias**. Then it squashes the total: a common squash, ReLU, turns anything below 0 into 0.

Think of it as a vote. Each input pushes the total up or down, as hard as its weight says, and the
neuron only passes a signal on when the total comes out above 0.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

A neuron does three things:

1. **Multiply** each input by its own <GlossaryPopover term="weight">weight</GlossaryPopover>. A big
   weight means "this input matters a lot"; a negative weight (dashed wire) means "this input
   pushes the other way".
2. **Add** the results together, plus one more number called the **bias**, which shifts the total
   up or down.
3. **Squash** the total with an *activation function*. ReLU, for example, turns any negative total
   into 0 and leaves positive totals alone. Without this step, stacking neurons would only ever
   produce straight lines, however many you used.

So a neuron is a small detector. The weights decide which mix of inputs it looks for, the bias how
much of that mix it needs, and the squash makes it stay quiet until it has enough: with ReLU, it
outputs 0 below that point and a bigger number the further past it the inputs go.

The idea is old. Warren McCulloch and Walter Pitts described a mathematical neuron in
1943,<Cite id="mcculloch1943" /> loosely inspired by brain cells, and Frank Rosenblatt's
*perceptron* of 1958 added weights that could be adjusted from examples.<Cite id="rosenblatt1958" />
Real brain cells are far more complicated; the name stuck anyway.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### The neuron as a formula

With inputs $x_1, \dots, x_n$, weights $w_1, \dots, w_n$, bias $b$, and activation function $f$, a
neuron outputs

$$
y = f\!\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i x_i + b\right), \qquad \operatorname{ReLU}(z) = \max(0, z)
$$

A whole layer is the same thing for every neuron at once: $h = f(W x + b)$, where row $j$ of the
matrix $W$ holds neuron $j$'s weights and $b$ is now a list of biases. That's why models are run
as matrix multiplications.

The squash is what makes layers worth stacking. Without it, two layers collapse into one:

$$
W_2 (W_1 x + b_1) + b_2 = (W_2 W_1)\,x + (W_2 b_1 + b_2)
$$

which is a single layer with weights $W_2 W_1$ and bias $W_2 b_1 + b_2$: still a straight line,
or a flat plane, however many layers you stack.

</At>

## Many neurons make a network

<At levels="simple">

One neuron answers one yes-or-no question about its inputs, so on a map of them it can only draw
one straight edge: on one side it fires, on the other it stays quiet. Many neurons side by side
make a **layer**, and a next layer can combine their answers into complicated shapes. Below, three
neurons and one output neuron fence off a triangle. Turn neurons off to see what fewer can do:

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

One neuron answers one question about its inputs: is this mix of them big enough? On a map of the
inputs, the answer changes along a straight line. It fires on one side and stays quiet on the other.
The power comes from numbers: put many neurons side by side in a **layer**, feed their outputs into
the next layer, and the network can pick out complicated regions.

Below, a layer of three neurons reads the same two inputs. Each one draws its own straight line and
fires only on the far side of it. A second layer, here a single output neuron, adds up what they say
with negative weights, so it stays above 0 only where none of them fire: inside a triangle. Turn
neurons off to see what fewer can do. One gives a single straight edge, two a wedge. Try it:

</At>

<LayerPlayground />

<At levels="simple">

A token's vector has 768 numbers rather than 2, so GPT-2's neurons pick out regions in a space
with 768 directions. Nobody can draw that, but each neuron still works just like the ones above.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Two inputs give a flat square you can draw. A neuron in GPT-2 reads 768 numbers, so it splits a
space with 768 directions instead, and the next layer combines thousands of those splits. Nobody
can draw that, but each neuron still multiplies, adds, and squashes like the ones above.

</At>

## Where neurons sit in a model

<At levels="simple">

GPT-2 small is built from 12 **blocks**, one after another. In each block, a part called attention
lets each token take in what came before it (<ChapterRef to="attention" />), and then a layer of
3,072 neurons works on each token's vector. After the last block, the vector becomes a chance for
every next token.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Here is the whole path through GPT-2 small, with the parts this course has covered and the ones
still to come:

1. Each token becomes a vector of 768 numbers (<ChapterRef to="embeddings" />).
2. The vectors pass through 12 **blocks**, one after another. In each block, attention lets every
   token take in information from the tokens before it (<ChapterRef to="attention" />), and then a
   layer of 3,072 neurons works on each token's vector on its own.<Cite id="gpt2-config" /> The
   two together make a transformer block (<ChapterRef to="the-transformer-block" />).
3. After the last block, each token's vector is turned into a chance for every possible next token.

Its neurons use GELU, a smooth cousin of ReLU, as their squash.<Cite id="hendrycks2016" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

In each block, GPT-2's neurons form two layers, a part called the MLP:

$$
\operatorname{MLP}(x) = W_2 \, \operatorname{GELU}(W_1 x + b_1) + b_2
$$

where $x$ is a token's 768 numbers, $W_1$ (3,072 × 768) and $b_1$ feed the 3,072 neurons, and
$W_2$ (768 × 3,072) and $b_2$ turn their outputs back into 768 numbers.<Cite id="gpt2-config" />
That's 4,722,432 weights and biases per block, and 56,669,184 across the 12 blocks: about 46% of
the whole model. GELU is $\operatorname{GELU}(z) = z\,\Phi(z)$, where $\Phi(z)$ is the chance that
a standard normal random number is below $z$. GPT-2 uses a faster approximation of it:

$$
\operatorname{GELU}(z) \approx \tfrac{1}{2} z \left(1 + \tanh\!\left(\sqrt{2/\pi}\,\left(z + 0.044715\,z^3\right)\right)\right)
$$

which matches the exact curve to within about 0.001 everywhere.<Cite id="hendrycks2016" />

</At>

## Who sets the weights?

<At levels="simple">

Every one of those neurons has weights, numbers like the ones on your sliders. So does the rest of
the model: the token vectors are numbers too. Above, someone chose the triangle's weights by hand.
Nobody could do that for millions of them.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Every one of those neurons has its own weights and bias, numbers like the ones on your sliders. So
does the rest of the model: the token vectors from <ChapterRef to="embeddings" /> are numbers too,
and so is attention. GPT-2 small has about 124 million in all. The triangle's weights above were
picked by hand to make a triangle. Nobody could pick 124 million, and nobody knows in advance which
patterns a language model needs its neurons to pick out.

</At>

<RecapCard>

- A language model is a calculation from token vectors to a chance for every next token, and much
  of that calculation is done by neurons.
- A neuron multiplies each input by a weight, adds them up with a bias, and squashes the result.
- Weights say how much each input matters, and in which direction.
- Layers of many neurons, stacked, can pick out complicated patterns. GPT-2 small has a layer of
  3,072 in each of its 12 blocks, after attention.

<At levels="deep">

- A layer computes $f(W x + b)$, one row of $W$ per neuron; without $f$, stacked layers collapse
  into one.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** nobody set GPT-2's 124 million numbers by hand. So how were they chosen?
